1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,933
In January 2025, in his inaugural
speech, the President of the United

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,167
States made a promise that
stunned an entire country.

3
00:00:10,233 --> 00:00:14,367
He said America was taking
the Panama Canal back.

4
00:00:14,433 --> 00:00:16,900
Panama's answer came fast.

5
00:00:16,967 --> 00:00:21,567
The canal belongs to Panama,
every square meter of it.

6
00:00:21,633 --> 00:00:23,733
But here is the strange part.

7
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:26,533
The United States built that canal.

8
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:28,900
It ran it for eighty-five years.

9
00:00:28,967 --> 00:00:32,967
And then, in 1999, it
handed the whole thing over.

10
00:00:33,033 --> 00:00:36,500
Panama did not pay a single cent.

11
00:00:36,567 --> 00:00:40,100
So why would the most powerful
country on Earth give away one of the

12
00:00:40,167 --> 00:00:43,033
most valuable pieces
of land on the planet?

13
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:45,733
And why does it want it back now?

14
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:50,133
To understand that, we need to go
back to a time when ships had to sail

15
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:53,000
around an entire continent.

16
00:00:53,233 --> 00:00:58,700
Before the canal, a ship going from New
York to San Francisco had to sail all

17
00:00:58,767 --> 00:01:03,167
the way around South America,
past the storms of Cape Horn.

18
00:01:03,233 --> 00:01:05,900
About thirteen thousand miles.

19
00:01:05,967 --> 00:01:08,967
But in one place, the
two oceans almost touch.

20
00:01:09,033 --> 00:01:13,333
A thin strip of jungle in
Panama, about fifty miles wide.

21
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:18,033
Cut through it, and the same trip
drops to around five thousand miles.

22
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:22,500
Whoever controlled that strip would
control one of the most important shortcuts

23
00:01:22,567 --> 00:01:23,667
on Earth.

24
00:01:23,733 --> 00:01:25,767
The idea was not new.

25
00:01:25,833 --> 00:01:32,000
In 1534, the King of Spain ordered a
survey to see if the isthmus could be cut.

26
00:01:32,067 --> 00:01:37,333
For almost four hundred years,
nobody managed to do it.

27
00:01:37,567 --> 00:01:40,133
The first to try were the French.

28
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:45,733
In 1881, Ferdinand de Lesseps, the
hero who had built the Suez Canal,

29
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:51,600
arrived in Panama with a bold plan: dig a
canal at sea level, straight through the

30
00:01:51,667 --> 00:01:53,167
mountains.

31
00:01:53,233 --> 00:01:55,333
The jungle had other plans.

32
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:58,400
Mudslides buried the work again and again.

33
00:01:58,467 --> 00:02:00,800
And then came the mosquitoes.

34
00:02:00,867 --> 00:02:04,433
Yellow fever and malaria
tore through the camps.

35
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:09,100
By most estimates, around
twenty-two thousand workers died.

36
00:02:09,167 --> 00:02:13,867
In 1889, the French company went
bankrupt, in one of the biggest

37
00:02:13,933 --> 00:02:16,633
financial scandals of the century.

38
00:02:16,700 --> 00:02:22,533
The great canal was left half-dug in
the jungle, waiting for someone else.

39
00:02:22,767 --> 00:02:25,700
That someone was Theodore Roosevelt.

40
00:02:25,767 --> 00:02:29,600
In 1903, Panama was not even a country.

41
00:02:29,667 --> 00:02:32,067
It was a province of Colombia.

42
00:02:32,133 --> 00:02:38,300
Roosevelt offered Colombia a deal for the
canal zone, and Colombia's senate said no.

43
00:02:38,367 --> 00:02:40,833
So Roosevelt found another way.

44
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:46,700
When rebels in Panama declared independence,
an American warship, the USS Nashville,

45
00:02:46,767 --> 00:02:49,133
was already waiting off the coast.

46
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:51,900
Colombian troops could not get through.

47
00:02:51,967 --> 00:02:56,367
Within days, the United States
recognized the new country.

48
00:02:56,433 --> 00:02:59,300
Two weeks later, a treaty was signed.

49
00:02:59,367 --> 00:03:03,700
And the man who signed for
Panama was not even Panamanian.

50
00:03:03,767 --> 00:03:06,900
He was a Frenchman from
the old canal company.

51
00:03:06,967 --> 00:03:12,767
America got a strip of land ten miles
wide, from ocean to ocean, forever.

52
00:03:12,833 --> 00:03:17,533
Panama got ten million dollars,
plus a small yearly payment.

53
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:21,733
Roosevelt later bragged
about it: I took the Isthmus.

54
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:27,900
Years later, in 1921, the United States
quietly paid Colombia twenty-five million

55
00:03:27,967 --> 00:03:30,833
dollars to make peace.

56
00:03:31,067 --> 00:03:34,667
This time, America started
with the mosquitoes.

57
00:03:34,733 --> 00:03:39,967
Army doctor William Gorgas drained
swamps, fumigated houses and covered

58
00:03:40,033 --> 00:03:41,400
water barrels.

59
00:03:41,467 --> 00:03:45,867
Within a few years, yellow
fever was gone from the zone.

60
00:03:45,933 --> 00:03:48,567
And the engineers changed the plan.

61
00:03:48,633 --> 00:03:52,533
Instead of cutting all the way down
to sea level, they built a giant

62
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:54,433
staircase of water.

63
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:58,733
Locks lift every ship eighty-five
feet up into an artificial lake,

64
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:03,267
carry it across the country, and
lower it back down on the other side.

65
00:04:03,333 --> 00:04:06,367
Most of the people who
dug it were not American.

66
00:04:06,433 --> 00:04:11,067
Tens of thousands came from the
Caribbean, especially from Barbados.

67
00:04:11,133 --> 00:04:13,533
And the zone had two payrolls.

68
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:16,133
White Americans were paid in gold.

69
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:20,533
Black Caribbean workers were
paid in silver, earned far less,

70
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:22,900
and lived in separate camps.

71
00:04:22,967 --> 00:04:27,967
It cost about three hundred and seventy-five
million dollars, and more than five

72
00:04:28,033 --> 00:04:30,833
thousand six hundred workers died.

73
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:36,633
In August 1914, the first ship crossed
from the Atlantic to the Pacific.

74
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:41,300
Crossing from one ocean to the
other now took about ten hours.

75
00:04:41,367 --> 00:04:46,333
And the Canal Zone became a little piece
of America in the middle of Panama.

76
00:04:46,400 --> 00:04:53,000
American police, American courts,
American schools, even American mail.

77
00:04:53,067 --> 00:04:59,033
Panamanians needed permission to cross
a strip of land in their own country.

78
00:04:59,267 --> 00:05:02,000
For decades, that anger grew.

79
00:05:02,067 --> 00:05:07,667
Then, in January 1964, Panamanian
students tried to raise their flag

80
00:05:07,733 --> 00:05:10,800
at an American high
school inside the zone.

81
00:05:10,867 --> 00:05:12,767
The flag was torn.

82
00:05:12,833 --> 00:05:15,700
Riots exploded along the border.

83
00:05:15,767 --> 00:05:21,567
When it was over, more than twenty Panamanians
and four American soldiers were dead.

84
00:05:21,633 --> 00:05:25,000
Panama cut diplomatic
ties with Washington.

85
00:05:25,067 --> 00:05:29,133
Panamanians still remember
that day as Martyrs' Day.

86
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:35,167
And American presidents started to realize
the zone was turning into a liability.

87
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:41,067
In 1977, President Jimmy Carter signed
two treaties with Panama's leader,

88
00:05:41,133 --> 00:05:42,833
Omar Torrijos.

89
00:05:42,900 --> 00:05:48,367
The deal: Panama would take full control
of the canal at the end of the century.

90
00:05:48,433 --> 00:05:51,367
Back home, it was a political firestorm.

91
00:05:51,433 --> 00:05:57,400
Ronald Reagan campaigned against it: we
bought it, we paid for it, it's ours.

92
00:05:57,467 --> 00:06:02,733
The Senate approved the treaties by
sixty-eight to thirty-two, just one vote more

93
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:04,100
than it needed.

94
00:06:04,167 --> 00:06:05,800
But there was a catch.

95
00:06:05,867 --> 00:06:10,600
A second treaty said the canal must
stay neutral, and it gave the United

96
00:06:10,667 --> 00:06:12,467
States the right to defend it.

97
00:06:12,533 --> 00:06:14,967
And Washington used its muscle.

98
00:06:15,033 --> 00:06:21,033
In 1989, twenty-seven thousand American
troops invaded Panama to remove

99
00:06:21,100 --> 00:06:23,733
the dictator Manuel Noriega.

100
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:30,133
Then, at noon on December 31st,
1999, control passed to Panama.

101
00:06:30,200 --> 00:06:34,067
After eighty-five years, the
canal belonged to Panama.

102
00:06:34,133 --> 00:06:35,633
For nothing.

103
00:06:35,867 --> 00:06:38,100
And Panama has run it well.

104
00:06:38,167 --> 00:06:41,800
Every single day, dozens
of giant ships cross it.

105
00:06:41,867 --> 00:06:45,900
The canal brings Panama around
five billion dollars a year.

106
00:06:45,967 --> 00:06:50,200
And the biggest customer, by
far, is the United States.

107
00:06:50,267 --> 00:06:53,300
But the canal has a weakness: water.

108
00:06:53,367 --> 00:06:57,367
Every ship that crosses uses around
fifty million gallons of fresh

109
00:06:57,433 --> 00:06:58,900
water from the lake.

110
00:06:58,967 --> 00:07:02,467
In 2023, a drought dried it up.

111
00:07:02,533 --> 00:07:07,667
Crossings were cut, more than a hundred
ships waited in line, and one company paid

112
00:07:07,733 --> 00:07:11,867
nearly four million dollars
just to skip the line.

113
00:07:11,933 --> 00:07:13,733
Then there are the ports.

114
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:18,533
Since 1997, the docks at both ends
of the canal have been run by a

115
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:20,600
company from Hong Kong.

116
00:07:20,667 --> 00:07:25,200
Washington says that gives China
a foothold on America's doorstep.

117
00:07:25,267 --> 00:07:29,433
And that is the real reason the
canal is back in the headlines.

118
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:34,967
In 2025, the Hong Kong company
agreed to sell those ports to a group

119
00:07:35,033 --> 00:07:39,233
led by the American giant BlackRock,
as part of a deal worth nearly

120
00:07:39,300 --> 00:07:41,833
twenty-three billion dollars.

121
00:07:41,900 --> 00:07:45,533
Beijing pushed back, and the
deal turned into a tug-of-war

122
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:47,833
between the two superpowers.

123
00:07:47,900 --> 00:07:51,300
And China has looked
at this region before.

124
00:07:51,367 --> 00:07:57,367
In 2013, a Chinese businessman won the
rights to dig a rival canal through

125
00:07:57,433 --> 00:08:01,367
Nicaragua, more than three
times longer than Panama's.

126
00:08:01,433 --> 00:08:02,900
It was never built.

127
00:08:02,967 --> 00:08:08,300
But it showed how valuable this
narrow strip of land still is.

128
00:08:08,533 --> 00:08:09,900
Look at the map again.

129
00:08:09,967 --> 00:08:11,533
Greenland in the north.

130
00:08:11,600 --> 00:08:13,433
Panama in the south.

131
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:17,900
Both sit on the routes that move the
world's trade and the world's navies.

132
00:08:17,967 --> 00:08:22,933
Control those points, and you
control who can move, and how fast.

133
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:27,900
America built the canal with its money
and its engineers, and it gave it back

134
00:08:27,967 --> 00:08:32,367
because holding it had become
more expensive than letting it go.

135
00:08:32,433 --> 00:08:35,033
Panama took it, and made it work.

136
00:08:35,100 --> 00:08:41,600
And Panama still says what its students
said in 1964: the canal is ours.

137
00:08:41,667 --> 00:08:47,267
Now the question is whether a promise
signed in 1977 still holds in a world

138
00:08:47,333 --> 00:08:50,667
where China is the other superpower.

139
00:08:50,900 --> 00:08:55,600
Every border on this map has a
price, and someone who paid it.

140
00:08:55,667 --> 00:08:59,200
If you want to see the other piece
of land America is trying to get,

141
00:08:59,267 --> 00:09:01,633
watch our video on Greenland next.

142
00:09:01,700 --> 00:09:06,300
And subscribe, so you don't
miss what's behind the next map.
